|term-group[binding][name][mint][maintainer][dangle][collide]
  :status proposed

  A **BINDING** is the first of the two mechanisms by which a @[reference] actually reaches its referents (the other is @[match]): someone deliberately associates a **NAME** with a referent and keeps the association alive. Everything about a binding — including both of its failure modes — is a fact about that upkeep, never about the name or the referent themselves.

  |term[name] A recognizable, repeatable token — a word, a string, a number.
    |invariants
      - Nothing about a name does any work beyond being recognizable wherever it is used

  |term[binding] A deliberate, maintained association from a name to a referent.
    :synonyms [declaration] ; the scope-graphs literature's word
    |rels :binds   @{name}       {1,1}
          :to      @{referent}   {1,1}
          :kept-by @{maintainer} {1,1}
    |invariants
      - The association is the whole connection; no property of the referent participates, and none of the name's beyond recognizability
      - Its failure modes are the maintenance's: @{dangle} and @{collide}, nothing else
      - Uniqueness of names is a norm a maintainer may police (cheapest at mint time), never part of this definition; unpoliced, a collision surfaces later as ambiguity at use

  |term[mint] The creation of a binding, at a moment, by its first maintainer.

  |term[maintainer] Whoever minted a binding or inherits its upkeep — a person, a family, a filesystem, a registry, a community.

  |term[dangle] The failure where a binding's upkeep is lost, or unknown where the name is used.

  |term[collide] The failure where one maintainer holds more than one mint of the same name.

  |discussion
    - Collision is never defined away: the refused-package incident shows real systems have collisions and are judged by how they surface them, and the scope-graphs calculus agrees — duplicate declarations stay representable precisely so that erroneous states (the normal case in tooling) remain describable. This is a deliberate divergence from a prior formulation that built "unique within parent" into the definitions.
    - "Joseph" in a large jurisdiction is not a weak name — it is a good binding whose maintaining community is too large for the job; that is why the rest of the address exists.
    - Where bindings live — the jurisdiction within which collisions are counted — stays informal until @[scope].
  |examples
    - A filename (maintainer: the filesystem)
    - A phone-book entry (maintainer: the publisher)
    - A variable declaration (maintainer: the language scope it lives in)
    - "Joseph" within a household (maintainer: the family)
    - A DNS record (maintainer: the zone's operator)
  |working-notes
    - Hardest known stress case, recorded before someone finds it against us: Java packages "exist by virtue of use" — the namespace is constituted by the union of its users, which strains "kept by whoever minted it."
